$DIR/main.p:16:2: cannot pass o to fmt.Println, which keeps it after returning
    o is an interface that points at a copy of its value in f's frame.
    Converting a value larger than one word to an interface puts a copy of the
    value in the frame of the function that converts it (here f, at main.p:16),
    and the interface points at that copy. fmt.Println passes a pointer loaded
    from args[i] to Fprintln (print_linux.p:17), which passes a pointer loaded
    from args[i] to (*printer).doPrint (fmt.p:948), which passes a pointer
    loaded from args[i] to (*printer).printArg (fmt.p:915), which passes arg to
    a formatting method of what it walks (which may call main.(*S).FmtList)
    (fmt.p:671), which stores s in package variable kept (main.p:7), so the
    interface would outlive f's frame.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Change fmt.Println to take a value or an owner, which it can keep: an
    owned[T] from new, a shared[T], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
